---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
// Landing page for people searching for an ArcGIS Online alternative.
// Facts are checked against the home page comparison, the in-portal
// help ("Coming from ArcGIS Online") and docs/migration/from-arcgis-online.md.
// Same writing rules as the home page: plain sentences, and say where
// ArcGIS Online is ahead.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { DEMO_URL, repoFile } from '../lib/site';

const terms = [
  ['Hosted feature layer, feature service', 'Data layer', 'Stored in PostgreSQL with PostGIS on your server.'],
  ['Web map', 'Map', 'Layers, a basemap and a starting view.'],
  ['Dashboard', 'Web app', 'Dashboards are web app layouts that read live from data layers.'],
  ['Experience Builder or Web AppBuilder app', 'Web app', 'A widget layout builder. Not a drop-in replacement.'],
  ['Survey123 survey', 'Form', 'Imports XLSForm. Not a drop-in replacement.'],
  ['Field Maps', 'Data collection and the field app', 'Offline capture and sync in the phone’s browser.'],
  ['Coded value domain', 'Pick list', 'Shared across layers and forms.'],
  ['User and Publisher roles', 'Contributor role', 'Viewer and Admin keep their names. A Data Editor becomes a Viewer with an edit share on the layer.'],
  ['Credits', 'None', 'Nothing is metered. You run the server.'],
];
---

<Base
  title="Open source ArcGIS Online alternative | GratisGIS"
  description="A free, open source alternative to ArcGIS Online that runs on your own server. See what matches, what does not, and how to move your content over."
  path="/arcgis-online-alternative/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'ArcGIS Online alternative', path: '/arcgis-online-alternative/' }]}
>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Compared with ArcGIS Online</p>
    <h1>An open source alternative to ArcGIS Online</h1>
    <p>
      GratisGIS is a GIS portal you install on your own Linux server. It covers the everyday work
      most teams use ArcGIS Online for: web maps, hosted data layers, sharing, forms, dashboards and
      offline field collection. It is free and open source under the AGPL, so there are no named
      users, no user types and no credits. This page says what lines up, what does not, and how to
      bring content across.
    </p>

    <h2>What you get</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Maps and data layers.</strong> Upload a shapefile, GeoPackage, file geodatabase,
        GeoJSON or anything else GDAL reads. Style it, set popups, and share it by group,
        organization or public link.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dashboards and web apps.</strong> Counters, charts, lists and maps that filter
        together and read live from the layer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Forms and field collection.</strong> A form builder, and an{' '}
        <a href="/field-data-collection/">offline field app</a> that runs in the phone’s browser.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Open standards.</strong> Every layer is served through OGC API Features and Tiles,
        so <a href="/qgis/">QGIS</a> and other tools read it with no connector.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your own sign-in.</strong> Accounts live in Keycloak on your server, with OpenID
        Connect for single sign-on.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The same things under different names</h2>
    <p>If you know ArcGIS Online, most of GratisGIS will be familiar. A few names changed.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="ArcGIS Online terms and GratisGIS terms">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">In ArcGIS Online</th>
            <th scope="col">In GratisGIS</th>
            <th scope="col">Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            terms.map(([them, us, note]) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{them}</td>
                <th scope="row">{us}</th>
                <td>{note}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Moving content from ArcGIS Online</h2>
    <p>
      An admin can run the importer from Admin, Migrations, From ArcGIS Online. It copies hosted
      feature layers into data layers on your server and rewrites your web maps to use them. You
      can also export a layer as GeoJSON, a file geodatabase or a shapefile and upload the file.
    </p>
    <p>
      The importer does not bring over apps, dashboards or surveys. Those you rebuild. The{' '}
      <a href={repoFile('docs/migration/from-arcgis-online.md')}>migration guide</a> lists each
      step and what survives the trip. Start with one team and one project before you move
      everything.
    </p>
    <p>The help has a page for each part of the move:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/help/coming-from-arcgis/terminology/">Terminology map</a>, term by term.</li>
      <li>
        <a href="/help/coming-from-arcgis/sharing-and-roles/">Sharing and roles</a>: user types,
        roles, groups and sharing levels.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/help/coming-from-arcgis/feature-service-to-data-layer/">Hosted feature layers</a>
        and <a href="/help/coming-from-arcgis/web-map-to-map/">web maps</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/help/coming-from-arcgis/field-maps-to-data-collection/">Field Maps</a> and{' '}
        <a href="/help/coming-from-arcgis/survey123-to-form/">Survey123</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/help/coming-from-arcgis/arcade-to-expressions/">Arcade</a> and the{' '}
        <a href="/help/coming-from-arcgis/analysis-tools/">analysis tools</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/help/coming-from-arcgis/app-builder-comparison/">Experience Builder, Web AppBuilder and dashboards</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where ArcGIS Online is ahead</h2>
    <p>Esri has had decades to build its platform. Stay with ArcGIS Online for now if you rely on:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Survey123’s advanced features, or Experience Builder apps you cannot rebuild.</li>
      <li>Story maps, hosted notebooks, or built-in routing and geoenrichment services.</li>
      <li>Native iOS and Android field apps. The GratisGIS field app runs in the browser.</li>
      <li>Enterprise single sign-on beyond OpenID Connect.</li>
      <li>A large network of training, partners and support contracts.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <p>
      The software is free. You pay for the server and the time to look after it. The public demo
      runs on 4 CPU cores and 8 GB of RAM, and adding people costs nothing. Installing is one
      command on a Linux server with Docker. See the <a href="/install/">install guide</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href={DEMO_URL}>Open the demo</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary btn-lg" href="/install/">Read the install guide</a>
    </div>

    <p class="fineprint">
      GratisGIS is an independent open source project. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by
      Esri. ArcGIS, ArcGIS Online, Survey123 and Experience Builder are trademarks of Esri.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
